  "insights": [
   "Corporate profit margins are at their highest level since 1947, while labor's share of output is at a record low.",
   "Real hourly compensation fell 0.1% over the past four quarters, despite a 2.2% rise in nonfarm business output per hour.",
   "Productivity gains have been the primary driver of margin expansion since 2020, rather than pricing power.",
   "Wage growth has lagged due to a shift in spending from labor to capital, nonlabor cost shocks, weaker worker bargaining power, and industry composition effects like the growth of lower-wage healthcare jobs.",
   "The Atlanta Fed's Wage Growth Tracker rose to 4.1% in August from 3.6% in June, suggesting wage pressures may be beginning to firm.",
   "If productivity growth remains strong, firms have sufficient margin buffer to absorb higher labor costs without necessarily passing them through to prices, potentially allowing real wages to recover without reigniting inflation.",
   "The Fed may not be finished with rate adjustments given inflation remains above target and financial conditions remain relatively loose."
  ],
