  "title": "Global Markets Daily: Credit: When Higher Yields Become Less Supportive",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-10-13",
  "insights": [
   "Credit spreads have historically cushioned rising government yields, but this correlation has shifted to positive since July, creating a dual headwind for unhedged credit returns.",
   "The breakdown in the negative correlation is driven by higher oil prices, rapid rates repricing, and renewed fiscal concerns in Europe.",
   "In USD IG credit, spreads now account for less than 15% of all-in yield, down from a 45% average between 2019 and 2021, reducing the buffer against rates volatility.",
   "The bank favors carry with controlled duration exposure, preferring USD over EUR credit, BBBs over AAs/As, and the belly of the curve over the long end.",
   "Spreads could tighten into year-end if rates volatility subsides and disinflation continues without a material growth deterioration."
